WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:05.000
Steps for Calculating a Performance Baseline

2
00:00:05.000 --> 00:00:09.566
Hello, Mike Harry. Welcome back where we’re again looking at establishing baselines.

3
00:00:09.566 --> 00:00:12.699
We’re going to start with an example.

4
00:00:12.700 --> 00:00:18.833
Now, let's consider a baseline. Step one. What does it take to do that?

5
00:00:18.833 --> 00:00:23.466
The very first thing that we've gotta do is determine the error rate.

6
00:00:23.466 --> 00:00:27.766
Now, what do we mean by an error? Well, let’s look at some examples.

7
00:00:27.766 --> 00:00:32.732
I think we're pretty clear at that, but this will help. We’ll look at some commercial industrial.

8
00:00:32.733 --> 00:00:37.766
Take banking, for example, or anywhere where people are waited on, a service.

9
00:00:37.766 --> 00:00:44.099
We see these people come and go, coming up to the desks, there to be waited on.

10
00:00:44.100 --> 00:00:50.966
Now, what can an error be? Well, an error could be that this individual right here,

11
00:00:50.966 --> 00:00:54.966
when the customer come up, pulled up the wrong account.

12
00:00:54.966 --> 00:01:00.032
Had to put it away and get the right account. That could be an error.

13
00:01:00.033 --> 00:01:05.933
Now let's consider another one, simple airbag.

14
00:01:05.933 --> 00:01:11.599
We see in airbag there deploying. Well, what might an error be?

15
00:01:11.600 --> 00:01:15.600
Pretty simple, if it doesn't deploy.

16
00:01:15.600 --> 00:01:21.000
Let's look again, at a facility.

17
00:01:21.000 --> 00:01:28.000
As we look at an industrial facility here. We’re going to zoom into the back dock.

18
00:01:28.000 --> 00:01:34.200
And what could an error be? An error could be a wrong delivery.

19
00:01:34.200 --> 00:01:41.200
Pretty simple. The truck pulls up and unloads the wrong materials.

20
00:01:41.200 --> 00:01:47.533
Now again, take something as simple as jumping.

21
00:01:47.533 --> 00:01:53.133
A person makes a jump.

22
00:01:53.133 --> 00:01:59.933
What can an error be? Well, pretty simple.

23
00:01:59.933 --> 00:02:05.399
They stumble and fall. That could be an error.

24
00:02:05.400 --> 00:02:10.333
Now what we’re going to do, is take and divide that error rate

25
00:02:10.333 --> 00:02:13.833
by the number of units produced.

26
00:02:13.833 --> 00:02:17.833
Thereby creating

27
00:02:17.833 --> 00:02:22.299
a unit error rate. Now remember, a unit can be anything.

28
00:02:22.300 --> 00:02:26.200
A unit could be a software program.

29
00:02:26.200 --> 00:02:31.433
A unit could be in the case of jumping, an event.

30
00:02:31.433 --> 00:02:36.466
A unit, in terms of delivery, could be the total order.

31
00:02:36.466 --> 00:02:40.466
A unit can be anything that we want it to be.

32
00:02:40.466 --> 00:02:46.066
Especially, when we're baselining. So, we’re looking at the error rate per unit,

33
00:02:46.066 --> 00:02:49.066
or what we call the unit error rate.

34
00:02:49.066 --> 00:02:54.632
Now step number two. Pretty straightforward.

35
00:02:54.633 --> 00:03:00.133
We take the unit error rate and we divide by the number of opportunities

36
00:03:00.133 --> 00:03:03.533
to get the opportunity error rate.

37
00:03:03.533 --> 00:03:08.533
Thank you for choosing SSMI.
